  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Wednesday: The main story for the next couple days\nwill be warming temperatures, along with hazy skies and areas of\nsmoke. An upper trough skirting across southern British Columbia\ntonight will help bring a north wind shift across northern\nWashington tonight into Monday. This is not a strong push, but\nenough in which the smoke models show smoke from the Kaiser Canyon\nand Modrite fires pushing south Monday morning into the Upper\nColumbia Basin and Spokane area. Then as the northerly winds go\nlight in the afternoon, the smoke and haze stalls in these same\nareas with afternoon mixing potentially bringing an increase in\nsmoke and haze to the Spokane area. Elsewhere smoke is expected to\ncontinue near wildfires. Then Monday night another slight wind\nshift out of the northeast may act to send smoke from these fires\ninto the western basin. On Tuesday and Wednesday Eastern WA/N\nIdaho becomes sandwiched between a low off the coast and high\npressure to our southeast. Broad southerly flow will bring\nadditional warming each day with highs in the 90s to near 100F.\nBut there are a few wild cards including mid level clouds as\nmonsoonal moisture begins to get draw up, as well as wildfire\nsmoke being sent northward from Oregon that could shave off a few\ndegrees. At minimum, widespread moderate HeatRisk is expected with\nareas of major HeatRisk possible on Wednesday.\n\nThursday through Sunday: A mid level short wave associated with \nthe low off the coast swings across the Inland NW. Moisture, lift,\nand elevated instability will be in place for elevated \nthunderstorm development Thursday into Friday. With precipitable \nwater values 150-170% of normal, some rain is expected with \nstorms, but the elevated nature of fast movement will make new \nfire starts a concern. Gusty outflow winds are also a concern with\nstorms. The forecast is still too far out to have much confidence\nabout amount of lightning, but at least isolated coverage is \nexpected. And following the lightning, there is good model \nagreement that the offshore low ejects inland as an open wave \nFriday into Saturday bringing with it drier, breezy, and more \nstable air. Although the precise timing carries greater \nuncertainty with which day. As it does come in, dry and breezy \nconditions are expected leading to the potential for rapid fire \nspread with any new fire starts. All in all, the combination of \nlightning followed by breezy winds is expected to lead to a period\nof critical fire weather conditions. This change will also bring \ntemperatures back down to seasonal values over the weekend. JW",
